1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:05,183
This exercise asks a simple question about
repository policy.

2
00:00:05,363 --> 00:00:07,629
Can an order have a quantity of zero?

3
00:00:07,809 --> 00:00:12,111
We want an answer that points to the
actual rule and the revision where it

4
00:00:12,111 --> 00:00:12,576
appears.

5
00:00:12,756 --> 00:00:17,171
The training index contains only synthetic
repository documents.

6
00:00:17,351 --> 00:00:22,279
We will trace a retrieved passage into the
answer, then check that passage directly.

7
00:00:23,757 --> 00:00:27,053
Begin with a few short passages from
approved files.

8
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:30,412
Attach each file path and revision to the
text.

9
00:00:30,592 --> 00:00:35,531
That metadata lets the reviewer check
relevance and freshness after retrieval.

10
00:00:35,711 --> 00:00:40,479
Include a passage about shipping so the
example has something nearby but

11
00:00:40,479 --> 00:00:41,141
unrelated.

12
00:00:41,321 --> 00:00:45,942
The size of the passage should preserve
enough neighboring text to explain the

13
00:00:45,942 --> 00:00:46,890
rule accurately.

14
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:53,243
An embedding model maps text into a list
of numbers that can be compared for

15
00:00:53,243 --> 00:00:53,953
similarity.

16
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:59,035
In this example, the system embeds the
question and the approved passages and

17
00:00:59,035 --> 00:01:00,499
compares their vectors.

18
00:01:00,679 --> 00:01:03,985
The point is to find useful candidate
evidence.

19
00:01:04,165 --> 00:01:09,299
Similarity alone cannot confirm that a
passage is current, applicable, or true,

20
00:01:09,299 --> 00:01:11,704
so keep the source metadata attached.

21
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:15,823
Look at the results before sending them
onward.

22
00:01:16,003 --> 00:01:19,946
The first passage addresses the quantity
rule and has a source path.

23
00:01:20,126 --> 00:01:24,789
The second passage discusses delivery
dates, so it adds no evidence for this

24
00:01:24,789 --> 00:01:25,341
question.

25
00:01:25,521 --> 00:01:30,627
A useful retrieval step returns enough
detail to support the answer and trims

26
00:01:30,627 --> 00:01:35,269
material that would distract the model or
obscure the relevant source.

27
00:01:36,715 --> 00:01:40,254
Now give the model the question and the
selected passage.

28
00:01:40,434 --> 00:01:45,106
This combination is retrieval augmented
generation, or R A G.

29
00:01:45,286 --> 00:01:48,479
Retrieval happens before the answer is
generated.

30
00:01:48,659 --> 00:01:54,034
The response cites the order policy and
applies the positive integer rule to zero.

31
00:01:54,214 --> 00:01:58,682
Open the cited file and confirm the answer
against the exact text.

32
00:01:58,862 --> 00:02:01,508
The citation is evidence you can check.

33
00:02:02,965 --> 00:02:07,274
Now repeat the exercise with the order
policy passage removed.

34
00:02:07,454 --> 00:02:11,017
The supplied material does not answer the
quantity question.

35
00:02:11,197 --> 00:02:15,975
A good response identifies the missing
evidence rather than guessing a rule.

36
00:02:16,155 --> 00:02:21,432
In a maintained repository, also check the
revision and refresh the index when

37
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:22,244
appropriate.

38
00:02:22,424 --> 00:02:27,098
These checks prevent an outdated or
unrelated passage from becoming a

39
00:02:27,098 --> 00:02:28,249
confident answer.

40
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:34,865
Before adopting retrieval for an actual
repository, confirm what may be indexed,

41
00:02:34,865 --> 00:02:38,984
where the index is stored, and which
identities can retrieve it.

42
00:02:39,164 --> 00:02:42,782
In this course we illustrate the pattern
with synthetic data.

43
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:48,081
The same habit that helps a developer
verify a code suggestion applies here.

44
00:02:48,261 --> 00:02:53,235
Record the evidence, confirm the source,
and say when the available material is

45
00:02:53,235 --> 00:02:54,053
insufficient.
